WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.570
Why are big pharma companies
paying billions of dollars

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.810
to take over, in some cases,
small companies that have just

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.850
one drug in their pipeline?

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.350
The answer is simple.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.410
Drug companies want to
maximise the value of each drug

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.650
in their portfolio for as long
as possible until the patent

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.770
runs out.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.520
But at some point, those
patents face a cliff.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.360
The competition is waiting to
rush in and make generic drugs

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.500
to treat the same thing.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.960
So what can drug companies do to
find the next blockbuster drug?

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.080
They can try to build
it in-house, or buy

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:44.830
the companies that hold the
promise of the next big drug.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.080
But big pharma has
generally faced pressure

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.440
from its investors to
cut its own R&amp;D spending.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.930
That leaves big pharma
with a decision to make.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.230
Do they risk buying a company
that has probably never

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.770
even brought a drug to
market, or do they wait?

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.380
The problem with waiting
is one of your competitors

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.480
may come and buy that company.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.610
That is why, over
the last five years,

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.900
we have seen big pharma
making very big bets.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.860
In 2015, AbbVie won a
three-way bidding war

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.330
to pay $21bn dollars to buy
Pharmacyclics, a cancer drug

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.150
maker that makes
literally one drug.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.070
There was Pfizer's takeover
of Medication in 2016.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:32.830
Also in 2016, AbbVie agreed to
pay $5.8bn to buy Stemcentrx.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.320
This frenzy of
dealmaking led investors

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.370
to start wondering whether big
pharma was paying too much.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.320
And that is why the
level of deal activity

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.650
dropped in 2017 and
for a lot of 2018,

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:47.590
but then things started
to pick right back up.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:52.510
GlaxoSmithKline said it would
pay $5.1bn to buy Tesaro.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:57.880
And Eli Lilly said it would
pay $8bn to buy Loxo Oncology.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.360
And then, there was one of the
biggest deals in M&amp;A history

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.400
across any asset class.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.430
Bristol-Myers Squibb said
it was acquiring Celgene

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:10.220
in an eye-watering $90bn deal.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:12.520
So are we set for
another big year

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.560
of pharmaceutical dealmaking?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.080
That appears to be the
case, as big pharma looks

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.310
ready to take big risks again.

